course: everyday
lesson: 243
title: Puzzles, money and video games
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A jigsaw puzzle is easier with a method: edges and corners first, then group the colours. Good games of all kinds need a plan and a calm head.
explain: Video games have age ratings (ESRB in North America, PEGI in the UK, Ireland and Europe) that warn about violence, scary content and online contact. Play games for your age, and ask a parent.
explain: Games can cost real money. Coins, skins (new looks for a character) and loot boxes (mystery prize boxes) are paid for with real money, so never buy anything without a parent's permission. Anyone offering free game coins for your password is a scammer trying to steal your account, and unknown downloads can carry viruses.
explain: Games can stir up strong feelings. When you lose a level again and again and feel angry, put the controller down, breathe and take a break; anger makes you play worse.
explain: Example: a boy in Dublin is asked in a game chat for his password in exchange for 10,000 free coins. He says no, tells his mum, and she helps him report and block the player.
fact: Start a jigsaw with the edges and corners, then group the colours; a method makes a hard puzzle easier.
fact: Do not gamble with real money; play for fun or points, because gambling with money is not allowed for children and can become a harmful habit.
fact: Video game age ratings, such as ESRB and PEGI, warn about violence, scary content and online contact, so play games rated for your age.
fact: Never buy coins, skins or loot boxes without a parent's permission, because in-game purchases cost real money.
fact: Never give your password to anyone except your parents; offers of free game currency for a password are scams.
fact: When you lose a hard level many times and feel angry, stop, breathe, take a break and come back later.
fact: Never throw a controller in anger; put it down and walk away for a moment, because devices break and people can get hurt.
fact: For motion games, clear space around you and wear the wrist strap, so you do not hit furniture or people or let the controller fly.
fact: If a game makes you dizzy or has flashing lights that bother you, stop playing and tell an adult; a few people have seizures (fits, when the body suddenly shakes or goes stiff) from flashing lights.
fact: When a younger sibling wants to watch a violent game, switch to a game suitable for them, because it can upset young children.
fact: On a shared console, use an agreed timer for turns and ask an adult if needed.
fact: Ask a parent before downloading a game or mod (a home-made add-on that changes a game), and use official stores, because unknown downloads can carry viruses.
exercise: How should you start a 500-piece jigsaw? | With the edges and corners, then group colours | With the middle pieces, one by one, working outwards | By pushing pieces together hard until they fit | By sorting all the pieces by size
exercise: Your friends want to bet their pocket money on a card game. What do you say? | Let us play for points or fun instead | Bet only a little, so it is fine | Bet, because winning money is good practice | Bet, but do not tell any adults
exercise: What does a PEGI or ESRB rating on a game tell you? | What age it is suitable for and what content it has | How many levels and secret worlds the game has in it | How good the reviews are | How much the game costs in the shops and online
exercise: A game says «Buy 500 gems now!» What should you do? | Not buy anything without a parent's permission | Press buy, because gems inside games are always free | Use your parent's card quickly before they notice | Buy them if you only press once
exercise: Why are in-game coins and skins not just play money? | They are bought with real money | They disappear when you turn off the game | They are given away free by the shop | They are only for grown-up players
exercise: A player says he will give you free coins for your password. What is it? | A scam to steal your account | A kind gift from a good player | A special prize for the best winners | A normal way to share an account
exercise: Who may know your game password? | Only your parents | Your best friend at school | Any player who asks nicely | A player who says he works for the game
exercise: You lose the same level for the tenth time and feel furious. What should you do? | Stop, breathe and take a break | Keep playing faster until you win | Shout at the screen to let it out | Throw the controller on the sofa
exercise: Why should you never throw a controller in anger? | It can break things or hurt people | It makes the game harder next time | The game can see you do it | It wastes the controller's battery
exercise: You are about to play a dancing game on the console. What should you do first? | Clear space around you and wear the wrist strap | Stand right next to the TV so that you can see better | Move the furniture closer to you | Hold the controller loosely so that your wrist can move freely
exercise: A game has lots of flashing lights and you feel dizzy. What do you do? | Stop playing and tell an adult | Play on with your eyes half closed | Turn the brightness up | Move closer to the screen
exercise: Your six-year-old sister wants to watch your game rated for teens. What do you do? | Switch to a game suitable for her | Let her watch, she will not understand | Tell her to cover her eyes in scary parts | Turn the sound off and keep playing
exercise: Your brother will not give up the console when it is your turn. What is the fair way? | Use an agreed timer and ask an adult if needed | Pull the plug out of the wall while he is playing | Grab the controller from his hands | Hide the controller tomorrow so he cannot play at all
exercise: You find a free game on a website you do not know. What should you do? | Ask a parent and use official stores | Download it quickly before the offer goes | Download it if the website says it is «virus free» | Download it on your parent's laptop instead of yours
test: A girl in Sydney is stuck on a puzzle with 1000 pieces of blue sky. What can help her most? | A method: edges first, then group by colour and shape | Forcing pieces together until they fit | Giving up, because big puzzles are impossible | Doing the whole puzzle upside down
test: A boy aged 9 wants a game rated PEGI 18. What is wise? | Choose a game rated for his age and ask a parent | Play it, because ratings are only advice for adults | Play it secretly at a friend's house | Play it with the sound turned off so it is less scary
test: A child spent $200 of her parents' money on game coins without asking. What is the lesson? | In-game purchases cost real money and need permission | Coins are free if the game says so | Only the big purchases need a parent's permission | Parents' cards are safe to use in games because games are not shops
test: Someone in a game chat says, «I am from the game company, send your password to keep your account.» What should you do? | Not send it, and tell a parent | Send it, because the company needs it | Send half of it to check it's really them | Send it and then change it later
test: Why does taking a break help after losing again and again? | Anger makes you play worse, and rest calms you | Games give you easier levels if you stop for a while | Breaks make the game forget all your mistakes | It does not help at all
test: Which is true about motion games? | You need space and a wrist strap so nothing gets hit | You should play them standing close to the TV | They are perfectly safe in a crowded room full of people | Wrist straps are only for small children
test: Why should a child not bet money on games, even small amounts? | It is not allowed for children and can become a harmful habit | Small bets always win in the end | Betting is only wrong on board games | Money always makes the game more fun for everyone who plays
test: A website offers a free mod that «unlocks everything». What is the danger? | It may carry a virus that harms the computer | It will make the game far too easy to enjoy | It will cost the game company money | There is no danger at all if the mod is free to download
test: guy online says free coins if i send my password. legit? | No, it's a scam, never give your password | Yes, free coins are real, loads of people get them | Yes, if he seems nice | Only send him half the password to be safe
test: can i buy the dragon skin, it's only 5 quid, mum's card's right here | No, ask a parent first, it's real money | Yes, game money isn't real | Yes, if it's under 10 pounds it doesn't count | Only if you pay her back later out of your pocket money
test: died on this level like 20 times i'm SO mad, wanna smash the controller | Put it down, take a break and come back later | Throw it on the sofa so it doesn't break | Keep playing till you win, no matter how long it takes | Shout at the screen and carry on
test: jigsaw has 1000 pieces, where do i even start | With the edges and corners, then the colours | In the middle, with the sky | With random pieces, keep trying until one fits | With the picture on the box turned upside down
explain: A boy in Ontario tapped "buy" again and again in a free game with his mum's card saved on the tablet; he spent over a thousand dollars in a week, and the family struggled to pay the bills.
explain: A girl in Australia gave her game password to a player who promised free skins; he stole her account and everything she had collected, and used it to trick her friends.
fact: In-game purchases spend real money, and small amounts add up fast.
fact: Anyone who asks for your password, even someone who seems friendly, can steal your account and trick your friends.
test: game wants mum's card for coins, she's asleep, only $2 | Don't use it, ask her first, it's real money | Use it, $2 doesn't matter | Use it n tell her later when she wakes up | Use dad's card instead, he won't notice it
test: online player says he'll give me free skins if i send my password | Don't, it's a trick to steal your account, tell an adult | Send it, free skins are rare | Send it, then change it tomorrow before he can use it | Send him half of it
test: Why did Sam's family get a huge bill from a free game? | He kept buying extras with the saved card | Free games charge for every hour you play them | The game broke the tablet | The bill was really from a different game his sister played
test: did u hear a kid spent like 2000 dollars on game skins with his mum's card and she cried? real lesson? | Never buy in-game items without a parent's permission | It's fine as long as the skins are really cool ones | In-game money isn't real money | Only buy when your mum's not looking so she doesn't get upset
test: apparently someone offered free V-bucks in a chat for ur password and a kid gave it and lost everything. what's the true lesson? | Never give your password; free currency offers are scams | Give it only to people with nice names | Change your password straight after you give it away | Free currency is real if it's in the official game chat
test: my mate says if u play games with flashing lights everyone gets fits. probably exaggerated? | Everyone is stretched; a few people do, so stop if bothered | Yes, every player gets a seizure | No, flashing lights never affect anyone | Yes, but only adults
exam: Identify the purpose of video game age ratings such as ESRB and PEGI. | To warn of violence, frightening content and online contact | To indicate how difficult a game is | To show how long a game lasts | To indicate the price of a game
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises in-game purchases? | They cost real money, and small sums accumulate rapidly | They are always entirely free of charge | They use only imaginary money that is never paid | They are refunded automatically at the end of each month
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice upon repeatedly failing a difficult level in anger. | One stops, breathes, rests and returns later | One throws the controller to relieve anger | One plays continuously until it is passed | One blames a sibling for the failure
exam: Ascertain the persons to whom a child may disclose his game password. | His parents only | Any friendly player online | Any person offering free currency | His classmates
